<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Howe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Howe was a Nova Scotian journalist and politician. As editor of the Novascotian he won a landmark libel case in 1835 that strengthened freedom of the press, and he led the reformers who achieved responsible government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> in 1848, the first in the British colonies overseas. He opposed Confederation and led a repeal campaign but joined the federal cabinet in 1869 and was briefly lieutenant governor in 1873.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-howe.jpg" alt="Joseph Howe" width="320" height="549" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Howe_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Howe was born at Halifax on 13 December 1804, the son of John Howe and Mary Edes. The family was of Puritan stock from Massachusetts; having stayed loyal to the crown in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, John Howe came to Halifax in 1779 as a Loyalist, set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> shop, became King's Printer in 1801 and deputy postmaster in 1803. Joseph attended the Royal Acadian School and served an apprenticeship in his father's shop. In 1828 he married Catherine Ann Susan McNab and bought the Novascotian, which he edited until 1841, reporting the assembly's debates himself and printing provincial literature and his own travel writing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The libel charge of 1835 arose from an anonymous letter, printed on 1 January, accusing Halifax politicians and police of pocketing £30,000 over thirty years. Since truth was no defence, Howe acted as his own lawyer and urged the jurors to leave an unshackled press as a legacy to their children; they acquitted him in 10 minutes. In 1840 John C. Halliburton, son of the presiding judge Brenton Halliburton, challenged him to a duel at Point Pleasant, where Howe fired into the air.</p><p>First elected in 1836 on a platform of responsible government, Howe formed a coalition with the Conservative leader James William Johnston in 1840 and was Speaker in 1841. After the Liberal victory of 1847, James Uniacke was premier of the new government of 1848, but many regarded it as Howe's ministry; he became Provincial Secretary and later the first Chief Commissioner of Railways, overseeing the start of the Nova Scotia Railway. He lost his seat to Charles Tupper in 1855, became premier when William Young was made a judge in 1860, and in 1863 left to become Imperial Fisheries Commissioner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Howe first attacked the Quebec Resolutions anonymously in the Botheration Letters, twelve editorials in the Morning Chronicle in 1865. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1867">British North America Act</a> of 1867, Nova Scotians elected 18 of 19 anti-Confederation members to the first Dominion Parliament. As secretary of state for the provinces from November 1869 he had a part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a>'s entry into Confederation. He died in office as lieutenant governor on 1 June 1873 and is buried in Camp Hill Cemetery in Halifax. His family published his Poems and Essays a year after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Howe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fishing, shipbuilding and timber drove the economy, and in 1848 <b>Joseph Howe's</b> reformers won responsible government.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halifax-nova-scotia">Halifax</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halifax-nova-scotia">Halifax</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.65,-63.57&amp;year=1848" aria-label="Halifax on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3033 585 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joseph Howe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3483 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3483 855h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.65,-63.57&amp;year=1848">What was here in 1848? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
